// Formative practice quiz for navigation. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/effects-routing-and-rendering/navigation

::navigation o1 - full reload::What is lost when a plain anchor navigates within a React app?{
=Everything in memory, including React state, because the app restarts#Correct. A full page load starts the JavaScript from scratch.
~Only the scroll position#Not quite. Much more is lost\: all in-memory application state.
~Nothing, because React restores it#Not quite. React cannot restore memory after a document reload.
~Only the URL#Not quite. The URL does change, but that is the part you wanted.
}

::navigation o1 - navlink purpose::What does NavLink add over Link?{
~It avoids a full page load, unlike Link#Not quite. Both avoid a full page load.
~It opens in a new tab#Not quite. It behaves like a Link. A new tab is a separate choice.
~It prevents the Back button from working#Not quite. It does not change how Back works.
=It knows whether it matches the current URL, for active styling#Correct. Its className and style can use the isActive value.
}

::navigation o2 - where to call::Where should the function returned by useNavigate be called?{
~Directly during render#Not quite. Render should be free of side effects.
=Inside an event handler or Effect#Correct. Navigation is a side effect, so it does not belong in render.
~In a different component from the Hook call only#Not quite. It can be called in the same component, in a handler.
~Only inside a Link#Not quite. A Link already navigates on its own.
}

::navigation o2 - replace::After a successful sign-in you navigate to the dashboard. Why pass the replace option?{
~So the dashboard loads faster#Not quite. Replace affects history, not speed.
~So the page fully reloads#Not quite. It does not reload the document.
=So Back does not return to the login form#Correct. Replace swaps the login entry in the history stack.
~So the URL does not change#Not quite. The URL does change to the dashboard.
}
